\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}

\title{The family of layered cubes}

\p{To pass from one cube size to another, we first fix a physical model.
Let #{C_N} be an #{N\times N\times N} array of unit cubies, with coordinates
#{(i,j,k)\in{1,...,N}^3}. A visible cubie is a surface cell, so at least
one coordinate is #{1} or #{N}. A cell with three boundary coordinates is a
\newvocab{corner}; with exactly two it is an \newvocab{edge}; with exactly
one it is a \newvocab{center}. This is a description of positions, before
we decide which labels a player can see.}

\p{The resulting counts are immediate: there are #{8} corners,
#{12(N-2)} edge cubies, and #{6(N-2)^2} center cubies. Their sum is the
number of surface cells. The count already explains why changing #{N} is
more than making a larger drawing. New edge and center positions appear,
and those positions can fall into different move orbits. Bonzio, Loi and
Peruzzi use this cubie decomposition in their general model
\citet{Sections 2--3}{bonzio2018nxnxn}; Salkinder gives a parallel orbit and
configuration analysis \citet{Section 2}{salkinder2021nxnxn}.}

\subtree{
  \title{Layer turns and the fixed frame}

  \p{Choose the six coordinate directions as a fixed frame. For an axis
  #{a\in{x,y,z}}, a depth #{r} with
  #{1\le r\le \lfloor N/2\rfloor}, and either boundary of that axis,
  turn the layer #{a=r} or #{a=N+1-r} by a quarter-turn. The turn rotates
  the other two coordinates and carries every sticker on a moved cubie with
  it. For odd #{N}, the central layer is deliberately omitted; this is the
  usual fixed-center convention. Thus #{N=3} has the six outer-face turns,
  while #{N=4} has the outer and adjacent inner layers.}

  \p{This choice distinguishes a layer turn from a whole-cube rotation.
  Whole-cube rotations change the chosen frame and are not generators of the
  state group here. They can be added as a separate symmetry action, or used
  to identify observations later. Keeping them out makes the six face
  directions, and hence a fixed center color scheme, part of the state
  description.}

  \p{A labelled state assigns a distinct name to every physical cubie and,
  when needed, to every sticker. A move acts by permuting these labels and
  their local frames. An ordinary colored observation forgets distinctions
  between equal-colored stickers; a marked or 'supercube' observation
  retains them. These are different quotients of the same labelled action.
  In particular, a statement that two center cubies 'look the same' is an
  observation claim, not a claim that the labelled pieces are interchangeable
  by a move.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{The coordinate signatures of the orbits}

  \p{The useful invariant is the distance of a coordinate from the nearest
  boundary. A layer turn permutes two coordinates, possibly changes their
  signs relative to the cube center, and leaves the multiset of these
  distances unchanged. It therefore preserves corners, edges and centers.
  On an edge, the one nonboundary coordinate gives a ring index. On a center,
  the two interior coordinates give an unordered pair of distances. Equal
  distances give a center-corner position in a concentric ring; unequal
  distances give a center-edge position. The central pair in an odd cube is
  the fixed center.}

  \p{Write #{K_e=N/2-1} for even #{N} and
  #{K_o=(N-3)/2} for odd #{N}. The position orbits under the selected layer
  turns have the following sizes. For even #{N}, the coupled-edge orbits
  #{E_1,...,E_{K_e}} each have #{24} positions. For odd #{N}, there is a
  single-edge orbit #{E_s} of size #{12}, together with coupled-edge orbits
  #{E_1,...,E_{K_o}} of size #{24} each. The term \em{coupled} records
  that the edge is away from the odd cube's middle edge position; it does not
  by itself choose an orientation convention.}

  \p{For even #{N}, each center-corner ring #{C_k},
  #{1\le k\le K_e}, has #{24} positions. The center-edge ring #{D_k},
  #{2\le k\le K_e}, has #{48(k-1)} positions, split into
  #{2(k-1)} distinct orbits of #{24}. For odd #{N}, there are #{6} fixed
  centers, center-corner rings #{C_k} of size #{24}, and center-edge rings
  #{D_k} of size #{24(2k-1)}, each for #{1\le k\le K_o}. Each odd ring is
  likewise a union of #{2k-1} orbits of #{24}. Summing these rows gives
  #{6(N-2)^2} centers in both parities. The formulas expose the distinction
  between a position orbit and an orientation coordinate: a one-sticker
  center has no orientation unless it is specially marked, while a coupled
  edge can have a convention-dependent orientation record.}

  \p{These sets are not merely a count. Face turns carry a representative
  edge around the four faces of its ring; an inner layer at depth #{k},
  conjugated by outer turns, carries it to the other axis directions. The
  same construction carries a center-corner representative around its ring
  and a center-edge representative around every position with the same
  ordered distance-pair or chirality label. Hence each displayed
  #{24}-position set is a move orbit, while the distance signature proves
  that different rows cannot mix. This is the coordinate part of the model;
  legality constraints on the permutations are a separate question.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{Small cubes make the parity split visible}

  \p{For #{N=2}, #{K_e=0}: there are only the eight corners. For #{N=3},
  #{K_o=0}: the pieces are eight corners, twelve single edges and six fixed
  centers. This is the familiar cube, but the description explains why its
  centers do not move in the chosen layer family.}

  \p{For #{N=4}, #{K_e=1}: there are eight corners, one coupled-edge orbit
  of #{24}, and one center-corner orbit of #{24}; there are no fixed centers
  and no center-edge ring. For #{N=5}, #{K_o=1}: there are eight corners,
  twelve single edges, one coupled-edge orbit of #{24}, six fixed centers,
  one center-corner orbit of #{24}, and one center-edge orbit of #{24}.
  The latter has #{8+36+54=98} visible cubies, the familiar Professor's
  Cube count. These examples show what changes with parity without claiming
  that the same labels or orientation coordinates are used by every source.}

  \p{The [ordinary cube action](frap-000P) is the #{N=3} outer-layer case of
  this model. The next chapter can therefore state constraints for a fixed
  three-layer convention, while the all-#{N} first law must still reconcile
  the labelled, colored and coupled-edge conventions in Bonzio--Loi--Peruzzi
  and Salkinder. Salkinder writes all slice rotations, including a central
  slice in odd size, and then treats its use through a shifted middle-slice
  convention; this is a genuine convention choice, not a harmless notation
  change. We use the fixed-center generators above and leave the translation
  between these move families explicit. A necessary invariant will not be
  promoted to a sufficiency theorem until that reconciliation is written out.}
}
